      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2013 The Authors Acta Obstetricia et Gynecologica Scandinavica © 2013 Nordic Federation of Societies of Obstetrics and Gynecology.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To estimate the risk of operative delivery according to maternal pre-pregnant body mass index (BMI) and gestational weight gain."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "DESIGN",
            "content": "Population-based pregnancy cohort study."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SETTING",
            "content": "The Norwegian Mother and Child Cohort Study."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SAMPLE",
            "content": "Term singleton deliveries in cephalic presentation, excluding preeclampsia, chronic hypertension, diabetes, gestational diabetes and placenta previa (n = 50,416)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Relative risks (RR) were obtained using general linear models."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
            "content": "RR of operative vaginal delivery and cesarean section."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Overweight and obese women had an increased risk of cesarean section, strongest for women with a pre-pregnancy BMI >40 (RR: 3.4, 95% confidence interval (CI): 2.8-4.1). There was also an increased risk of vacuum extraction delivery for women with a pre-pregnancy BMI >40 (RR: 1.5, 95% CI: 1.04-2.2). Women with a gestational weight gain of ≥16 kg had a significantly increased risk of forceps, vacuum extraction and cesarean section (RR: 1.2, 95% CI: 1.03-1.4, RR: 1.2, 95% CI: 1.1-1.23 and RR: 1.3, 95% CI: 1.26-1.4, respectively). Weight gain during pregnancy was significantly lower in obese women, but the children tended to be larger."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Obese women have an increased risk of operative delivery with vacuum extraction and cesarean section. Independently of pre-pregnancy BMI, we found an increased risk of operative intervention during delivery for women with gestational weight gain above 16 kg."
          }
        ]
      },
